WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:06.360
From an MCU adventure to a live-action adaptation&nbsp;
of a children's cartoon, these movies avoided R&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:11.640
ratings by ditching scenes that were overly&nbsp;
gory, too explicit, or simply in poor taste.

00:00:11.640 --> 00:00:16.260
There are only a few big DC heroes that you&nbsp;
can easily imagine in an R-rated feature,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:21.360
but Batman is definitely among them. He's a&nbsp;
gritty character who operates in a dreary city&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:26.400
fighting underworld criminals, but all his movies&nbsp;
— even the Christopher Nolan ones — have always&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:31.200
been PG-13 or tamer. When director Zack Snyder&nbsp;
paired the Dark Knight with the Man of Steel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:35.400
in "Batman v Superman: Dawn of Justice,"&nbsp;
though, he wanted to go darker than ever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:39.840
before. If Snyder had gotten his way, "Batman&nbsp;
v Superman" would have been even more mature.

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:47.020
"Tell me… do you bleed?"

00:00:47.020 --> 00:00:50.520
Snyder has spoken before about extended&nbsp;
versions of certain scenes in the movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:53.940
that would have earned it an R rating.&nbsp;
He revealed to Collider that there were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.940 --> 00:00:57.750
some violent moments that had to be trimmed,&nbsp;
like the opening. The director clarified —

00:00:57.750 --> 00:01:01.434
"The opening of the movie, the North&nbsp;
African sequence, is really much different."

00:01:01.434 --> 00:01:06.000
These deleted moments weren't seen in theaters&nbsp;
— but they did show up in the Ultimate Cut of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:11.580
the film, which was released on Blu-ray and DVD.&nbsp;
Speaking to Entertainment Weekly, star Ben Affleck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.580 --> 00:01:15.600
said he was completely in favor of the more&nbsp;
violent version being made available, saying —

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:17.460
"Why not have an R-rated version that you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.460 --> 00:01:20.520
release for download or on DVD&nbsp;
later? I think that's smart."

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:25.500
Set during a wild post-graduation party,&nbsp;
"Can't Hardly Wait" follows a group of teens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:31.080
as they look for love, sex, and revenge. With&nbsp;
some edgy late-'90s gags and a PG-13 rating,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.080 --> 00:01:35.940
it's not quite a sex comedy — but it's also&nbsp;
not exactly family friendly. Either way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:40.620
it was almost rated R, and there was plenty left&nbsp;
on the cutting room floor after the studio made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.620 --> 00:01:46.200
the last minute decision to help it get a wider&nbsp;
audience. Actor Seth Green told Vulture in 2015 —

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:49.500
"There was like 40 minutes of that&nbsp;
movie that was cut out. The movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:52.920
was originally an R-rated movie.&nbsp;
Sony, in the midst of putting it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:56.280
out — it was at the very beginning of&nbsp;
the reemergence of the teen comedy — was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.280 --> 00:01:59.520
scared. [...] Everyone was really kind of&nbsp;
concerned about what it was going to be."

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:03.480
According to the actor, CGI was used&nbsp;
to delete a beer bong in one scene,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.480 --> 00:02:06.420
while a sequence involving weed&nbsp;
brownies had to be removed.

00:02:06.420 --> 00:02:08.760
Some deleted scenes may even help explain other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.760 --> 00:02:12.870
odd moments in the PG-13 version&nbsp;
of the movie. Green continued —

00:02:12.870 --> 00:02:16.800
"[Jason] Segel's got a watermelon that he's&nbsp;
filled with vodka and kept in his freezer for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:20.700
all of senior year, and then he brings&nbsp;
it out to celebrate. Everybody goes for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.700 --> 00:02:24.840
it, and it shatters on the ground. So that's why&nbsp;
he's eating watermelon for the rest of the movie."

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:29.700
It's one thing when an R-rated sex comedy is&nbsp;
cut down to get a softer rating like "Can't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:33.240
Hardly Wait." It's much more rare, though,&nbsp;
for a film that's designed to be a family&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.240 --> 00:02:38.160
comedy — one based on an iconic children's&nbsp;
cartoon, no less — to contain R-rated moments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:42.720
that had to be pulled. If writer James Gunn and&nbsp;
actor Sarah Michelle Gellar are to be believed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:47.340
though, that's exactly what happened with the&nbsp;
2002 live-action adaptation of "Scooby-Doo."

00:02:47.340 --> 00:02:51.660
According to Gunn, the movie was originally&nbsp;
slapped with an R rating by the MPAA,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.660 --> 00:02:55.920
and plenty of changes had to be made.&nbsp;
One scene that was deleted depicted a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.920 --> 00:03:00.840
soul-swapped Daphne and Velma locking lips. As&nbsp;
Gellar confirmed on "Watch What Happens Live" —

00:03:00.840 --> 00:03:04.620
"There was an actual kiss between&nbsp;
Daphne and Velma that got cut. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.620 --> 00:03:07.020
feel like the world wants to see&nbsp;
it. I don't know where it is."

00:03:07.020 --> 00:03:10.380
That's not all — as Gellar tells it,&nbsp;
there was also a scene removed that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.380 --> 00:03:14.067
raised questions over Fred's&nbsp;
sexuality. She elaborated —

00:03:14.067 --> 00:03:18.879
"There's always been an implication about Fred&nbsp;
being interested in both parties. It all got cut."

00:03:18.879 --> 00:03:21.180
"He planned this somehow, didn't he?"

00:03:21.180 --> 00:03:23.838
"Hey, good lookin'!"

00:03:23.838 --> 00:03:28.320
When Jim Carrey came to fame in the mid-'90s,&nbsp;
it was for a string of vulgar comedies that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:33.000
included "The Mask" and "Ace Ventura: Pet&nbsp;
Detective." Though they were all rated PG-13,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:38.040
they pushed the envelope as far as they could with&nbsp;
jokes that stretched the boundaries of good taste.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:42.960
For the 1994 Farrelly brothers classic "Dumb&nbsp;
and Dumber," one scene was filmed that pushed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:46.920
the movie's inappropriateness levels&nbsp;
way over the edge. Watching it back now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.920 --> 00:03:49.800
it's pretty easy to understand&nbsp;
why it was made much shorter.

00:03:49.800 --> 00:03:54.180
The moment comes at a truck stop, when Harry and&nbsp;
Lloyd stop to refuel their vehicle and stretch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.180 --> 00:03:58.740
their legs. Lloyd goes to use the men's room and&nbsp;
spots a little bit of graffiti scrawled on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.740 --> 00:04:04.980
stall that promises some "manly love" at 2:15&nbsp;
AM. Noticing it's exactly 2:15 at that moment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.980 --> 00:04:09.360
Lloyd turns around to find Sea Bass entering&nbsp;
the men's room. The scene made it into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:12.840
movie's final cut — but what didn't are&nbsp;
some of the violent statements that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.840 --> 00:04:16.320
trucker makes towards Lloyd. The rest of&nbsp;
the film may have been full of gross-out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:20.760
gags and over-the-top vulgarity, but&nbsp;
this explicit threat of sexual assault&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:24.300
and murder would have no doubt pushed&nbsp;
the picture into R-rated territory.

00:04:24.840 --> 00:04:29.640
Steven Spielberg's "Jaws" might not be classified&nbsp;
as a horror movie by today's standards, but it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.640 --> 00:04:34.860
definitely had some spine-tingling scares that&nbsp;
had audiences in 1975 jumping out of their seats.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.860 --> 00:04:38.820
Despite being centered around a bloodthirsty&nbsp;
shark that's menacing a coastal community,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.820 --> 00:04:42.600
it might surprise younger film fans who&nbsp;
haven't yet seen it that the film is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.600 --> 00:04:48.180
actually rated PG and contains very little actual&nbsp;
bloodshed. Most of the deaths are off-screen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.180 --> 00:04:52.980
and the shark itself isn't even seen clearly very&nbsp;
often. In many ways, it's that lack of gore that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.980 --> 00:04:56.760
contributes to the film's scare factor — but&nbsp;
if the movie had been released as originally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.760 --> 00:05:01.020
intended, it would have included at least one&nbsp;
graphic moment likely warranting an R rating.

00:05:01.020 --> 00:05:04.860
In the movie, Chief Brody's son Michael and&nbsp;
his friends are out sailing when their boat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.860 --> 00:05:09.360
is overturned by the shark. Coming to their&nbsp;
aid is a man in a paddleboat — but in what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:12.540
would have been the most jaw-dropping&nbsp;
moment in the movie, the shark emerges&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.540 --> 00:05:16.620
out of nowhere and devours the man, dragging&nbsp;
him under as he clings to a screaming child.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.620 --> 00:05:21.134
In a behind-the-scenes featurette, actor Ted&nbsp;
Grossman, who played the victim, admitted —

00:05:21.134 --> 00:05:24.734
"It was just so violent, so&nbsp;
horrendous, that they couldn't use it."

00:05:24.734 --> 00:05:28.860
Spielberg agreed, saying that it was "too&nbsp;
bloody" and that the scene was "in bad taste."

00:05:28.860 --> 00:05:30.720
"I'm not gonna waste my time arguing with a man&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.720 --> 00:05:33.120
who's lining up to be a hot lunch.&nbsp;
I'm gonna see you later, Brody."

00:05:33.120 --> 00:05:37.920
"Indiana Jones and the Temple of Doom" is famously&nbsp;
one of the films that prompted the creation of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.920 --> 00:05:42.840
PG-13 rating, as some parents were outraged&nbsp;
at some of the violence in the PG-rated flick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.840 --> 00:05:47.640
during test screenings. The action-packed prequel&nbsp;
sees the whip-wielding relic hunter in India,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.640 --> 00:05:53.280
facing off against a demonic cult that engages in&nbsp;
human sacrifice. The most infamous scene involves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.280 --> 00:05:57.420
the cult's leader tearing the still-beating&nbsp;
heart out of a man's chest and holding it aloft.

00:05:57.420 --> 00:06:02.280
The final cut is pretty violent, but it was&nbsp;
almost even worse — a number of scenes were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.280 --> 00:06:06.660
removed from the film, presumably so it&nbsp;
could avoid an R rating. One depicted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.660 --> 00:06:11.160
a Thuggee torturer who's seen brutalizing a&nbsp;
group of slave children before he himself is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.160 --> 00:06:15.960
burned by lava in grisly fashion. Reporting&nbsp;
on the uproar surrounding the film, The New&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.960 --> 00:06:20.220
York Times revealed that censors in the United&nbsp;
Kingdom were horrified that "Indiana Jones and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.220 --> 00:06:25.470
the Temple of Doom" had managed to get a PG rating&nbsp;
in the States. Journalist Aljean Harmetz wrote —

00:06:25.470 --> 00:06:29.640
"James Ferman, secretary of the British board,&nbsp;
said that 'Indiana Jones,' as it stands,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.640 --> 00:06:32.460
could not even receive the&nbsp;
more restrictive 15 rating,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.460 --> 00:06:37.380
which prohibits children under age 15 from&nbsp;
seeing the film. To get a 15 rating, he said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.380 --> 00:06:42.240
a scene depicting 'the slow burning to death of&nbsp;
a man who is in absolute agony' must be trimmed."

00:06:42.240 --> 00:06:46.860
In 2022, "Black Adam" was supposed to&nbsp;
change the hierarchy of power in the DC&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.860 --> 00:06:51.660
Extended Universe with the arrival of Dwayne&nbsp;
Johnson as the titular anti-hero. Instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.660 --> 00:06:56.700
the movie bombed at the box office, and James&nbsp;
Gunn and Peter Safran's DC reboot was announced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.700 --> 00:07:00.840
soon after. Unlike Gunn's R-rated redo of&nbsp;
"The Suicide Squad" from the year prior,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.840 --> 00:07:04.680
though, "Black Adam" had some scenes removed&nbsp;
and altered to tone down the blood and gore.

00:07:04.680 --> 00:07:07.020
"Heroes don't kill people."

00:07:11.040 --> 00:07:12.000
"Well, I do."

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:16.080
According to producer Hiram Garcia,&nbsp;
"Black Adam" was originally rated R,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.080 --> 00:07:22.380
and some scenes had to be axed in order to get the&nbsp;
PG-13 rating the studio wanted. He told Collider —

00:07:22.380 --> 00:07:25.260
"We really wanted to make sure that&nbsp;
we honored the character of Black&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.260 --> 00:07:28.500
Adam. One of the things he's known&nbsp;
for is his aggression and violence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.500 --> 00:07:32.820
and to do a Black Adam movie that didn't have&nbsp;
that just wouldn't have been authentic. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.820 --> 00:07:35.940
we always went into this knowing that we&nbsp;
were going to push it as far as we did."

00:07:35.940 --> 00:07:39.840
The only deleted scene that has come to&nbsp;
light so far was discussed by VFX artists&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.840 --> 00:07:44.940
Nikos Kalaitzidis and Greg Teegarden in&nbsp;
an interview with Befores &amp; Afters. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.940 --> 00:07:48.780
described a moment where Black Adam violently&nbsp;
tears an arm from a mercenary soldier,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.780 --> 00:07:52.620
and it spasms after hitting&nbsp;
the ground. Teegarden joked —

00:07:52.620 --> 00:07:55.140
"You can't have a disembodied hand laying on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.140 --> 00:07:57.420
ground and the finger just&nbsp;
all of a sudden twitching."

00:07:57.420 --> 00:08:01.860
It's rare that the "Star Trek" franchise&nbsp;
goes too far with its content — but in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.860 --> 00:08:05.880
the series' second big screen outing, the&nbsp;
creators almost went overboard with one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.880 --> 00:08:09.720
particular scene. The film that remains the&nbsp;
most beloved in the franchise, "Star Trek II:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.720 --> 00:08:14.640
The Wrath of Khan," saw Ricardo Montalbán's&nbsp;
genetically augmented super tyrant return&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.640 --> 00:08:19.260
from exile and seek revenge on Captain Kirk.&nbsp;
There was an entire character removed from a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.260 --> 00:08:22.980
key subplot that involved Khan's quest for&nbsp;
vengeance, though — the villain's child.

00:08:22.980 --> 00:08:27.600
The toddler was originally supposed to pop up&nbsp;
at the climax of the film, when Khan detonates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.600 --> 00:08:32.460
the Genesis Device in a last ditch effort to kill&nbsp;
Kirk. In a deleted scene, the device sucks the kid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.460 --> 00:08:37.560
into its vortex before destroying the entire ship.&nbsp;
The full scene has never surfaced, but images&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.560 --> 00:08:43.380
from the sequence were published in StarBlazer&nbsp;
magazine in 1982. With the horrific death of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.380 --> 00:08:48.240
an infant at the center of this major scene, it's&nbsp;
not hard to see why the whole thing was scrapped.

00:08:48.240 --> 00:08:50.940
When one conjures up an image&nbsp;
of an R-rated Marvel movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.940 --> 00:08:55.860
"Thor" probably isn't the hero that springs to&nbsp;
mind. However, according to actor Christian Bale,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.860 --> 00:09:00.000
who played the villainous Gorr the God Butcher&nbsp;
in "Thor: Love and Thunder," there are actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:03.840
a series of deleted scenes that would have&nbsp;
made the Thunder God's fourth solo outing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.840 --> 00:09:09.300
an R-rated affair. Regarding his collaboration&nbsp;
with director Taika Waititi, Bale told Inverse —

00:09:09.300 --> 00:09:12.840
"With 'Thor: Love and Thunder,' Taika and&nbsp;
me, we kind of knew that some of the stuff&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.840 --> 00:09:16.980
we were doing probably wouldn't end up in the&nbsp;
film, but we wanted to just push it and see."

00:09:16.980 --> 00:09:21.720
So what happened? Despite their wish to push the&nbsp;
boundaries of what could appear in an MCU movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.720 --> 00:09:27.330
both Bale and Waititi eventually agreed that the&nbsp;
scenes they shot weren't right. Bale continued —

00:09:27.330 --> 00:09:31.800
"Ultimately, it is and should be a film that all&nbsp;
the family can go and enjoy. And Chris Hemsworth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.800 --> 00:09:34.980
a couple of times he looked at me it&nbsp;
was like, 'Dude, that's a little too&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.980 --> 00:09:38.340
far. I don't think anyone's gonna want to&nbsp;
see that unless it's an R-rated film.'"

00:09:38.340 --> 00:09:40.380
"We really need to stop meeting like this."

00:09:41.580 --> 00:09:42.114
"Call the ax."

00:09:42.114 --> 00:09:43.020
"I'll call the ax when you call the dentist."

00:09:43.020 --> 00:09:45.600
Despite those moments not making it to theaters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.600 --> 00:09:49.380
Bale says he has no regrets since he&nbsp;
had a great time filming regardless.

00:09:49.920 --> 00:09:54.360
Based on a series of young adult novels,&nbsp;
the "Divergent" films follow Tris Prior,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.360 --> 00:09:58.980
a defiant young woman in a dark future where&nbsp;
people are segregated based on their virtues.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.980 --> 00:10:04.080
When she joins the Dauntless faction due to her&nbsp;
bravery, she realizes that she is different from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.080 --> 00:10:08.220
the rest — and she must keep her unique nature&nbsp;
a secret as the world teeters on the brink&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.220 --> 00:10:12.720
of war. Squarely aimed at tweens and teens,&nbsp;
the first movie in the franchise had to cut&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.720 --> 00:10:17.220
some moments that were deemed too harsh for&nbsp;
the PG-13 rating the studio was aiming for.

00:10:17.220 --> 00:10:21.780
Despite being directly adapted from a sequence&nbsp;
in the book, it was decided that the scene in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.780 --> 00:10:26.460
"Divergent" in which Dauntless rookie Edward is&nbsp;
stabbed in the eye was simply too brutal to show.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.460 --> 00:10:30.240
Though it did find its way onto the deleted&nbsp;
scenes portion of the home video release,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.240 --> 00:10:35.220
its removal from the actual film clearly&nbsp;
disappointed actor Ben Lamb, who considered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.220 --> 00:10:39.720
his character's brutal disfigurement a highlight&nbsp;
of the production. Lamb told Collider —

00:10:39.720 --> 00:10:43.380
"The scene that I really enjoyed the most&nbsp;
was the eye stabbing. It's very gruesome."

00:10:43.380 --> 00:10:47.220
According to director Neil Burger, the&nbsp;
eye stabbing scene was actually removed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.220 --> 00:10:51.120
to fix the flow of the movie rather&nbsp;
than to help it get a PG-13 rating.

